  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 20.0% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously. FatigueIndex reached 0.6001 against a hard ceiling of 0.40 (excess: +0.2001, ~50% over limit), and ambient Noise reached 84.9 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB (excess: +4.9 dB). Because both violations are classified as human-centric under the CBPA governance policy, the schedule was automatically rejected and escalated. No feasible schedule exists at current demand levels without either reducing throughput or obtaining a temporary, time-bounded relaxation of one or both limits. Immediate manager action is required to unblock production.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Partial Demand Fulfilment with Split Shifts (Preserve All Human Constraints): Cap daily throughput so that both FatigueIndex and Noise remain within their original limits. Redistribute the 20% demand overage across an extended planning horizon using a third shift or weekend buffer run. No constraint waivers are issued; worker welfare is fully protected. Requires commercial negotiation with customers on lead times for the deferred volume.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Engineering Noise Controls with Temporary Noise Limit Waiver (Relax Noise Only): Deploy acoustic enclosures and mandatory PPE (rated hearing protection) on the three highest-noise workstations within 24 hours. With controls in place, request a time-bounded waiver raising the operational Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB for a maximum of 14 days while a permanent engineering solution is procured. FatigueIndex must remain at or below 0.40 via a strict rotation schedule; overtime is capped. This closes roughly 75% of the demand gap while limiting residual delivery shortfall to ~5%.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Dual Temporary Waiver with Compensatory Controls (Relax Both Constraints, Highest Risk): Issue a 7-day dual waiver: raise FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB, contingent on mandatory compensatory measures — additional 10-minute micro-breaks every 90 minutes, continuous physiological fatigue monitoring via wearables, audiometric spot-checks at shift end, and automatic production halt if FatigueIndex exceeds 0.55 intra-shift. This option closes the full demand gap but carries the highest regulatory and worker-safety risk; it requires sign-off from both the EHS officer and plant director, and must be reviewed after each shift. Waiver must not be renewed without a new feasibility assessment.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
