  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency (maximize, no upper bound)
       Q: Is there a ceiling on any individual resource dimension — for example, a maximum energy draw of X kWh or a maximum machine speed of Y% of rated capacity — beyond which the optimizer must not go, even if fatigue and noise constraints are still satisfied? Without a cap, the optimizer may legally increase power consumption by ~40% to squeeze marginal efficiency gains.
       A: Cap machine_speed_setpoints at 100% of manufacturer-rated speed and apply no additional energy ceiling; the hard constraints on fatigue and noise are treated as the sole binding limits on resource escalation.
    2. operator_fatigue (instantaneous vs. cumulative measurement)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue threshold an instantaneous ceiling that must never be crossed at any 5-minute reading, or is it an end-of-shift cumulative limit — and if instantaneous, should the optimizer apply a tighter intra-shift budget (e.g. ≤ 0.3 for the first 6 hours) to leave headroom before the shift ends?
       A: Treat the threshold as a hard instantaneous ceiling (≤ 0.4 at every 5-minute control step) with no additional intra-shift budget reserve; the optimizer may run operators at up to 0.4 throughout the entire shift.
    3. resource_efficiency (multi-resource weighting: energy vs. material vs. labour)
       Q: When trading off between resource types — for instance, running machines faster (more energy, less labour-hours) versus slower (less energy, more labour-hours) — should each resource be weighted equally in the efficiency denominator, or do you want energy, raw material, and labour to carry specific relative weights (e.g. 50%, 30%, 20%)?
       A: Normalise each resource to its shift-level budget and weight equally (1/3 each) in the composite denominator, using MES/ERP baselines as the normalisation reference.
    4. ambient_noise_level (Leq averaging vs. instantaneous peak)
       Q: The 80 dB(A) limit is implemented as a 5-minute Leq (equivalent continuous level), which permits short bursts well above 80 dB provided the average stays under the limit. Should there also be a peak constraint — for example, no single instantaneous reading above 85 dB(A) — to match the EU Directive 2003/10/EC peak action level?
       A: Apply only the Leq constraint (≤ 80 dB(A) per 5-minute window) with no separate instantaneous peak limit, consistent with the stated noise_threshold_source.
    5. operator_task_rotation_schedule (minimum rotation interval)
       Q: Since task rotation is a decision variable updated every 5 minutes, should a minimum continuous task duration be enforced (e.g. no rotation more frequently than every 30 minutes) to avoid disruptive micro-rotations that the fatigue sensors may not capture but that degrade skill continuity and productivity?
       A: Allow rotation at any control step (every 5 minutes) with no minimum dwell time; the optimizer may rotate freely as long as the fatigue and noise constraints are met.

