  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / assumptions.resource_fungibility
       Q: What are the monetary (or normalised) conversion weights used to aggregate energy (kWh), raw materials (kg), and operator labour-hours into a single resource-cost denominator — for example, is 1 kWh weighted equally to 1 labour-hour, or do you have a standard cost-per-unit for each input? Without explicit weights the optimiser can legally inflate the efficiency ratio by arbitrarily cutting cheap resources while consuming expensive ones.
       A: Apply site-average cost rates: energy at local grid tariff ($/kWh), raw materials at standard purchase cost ($/kg), and labour at loaded hourly wage ($/h); sum to a single cost-denominator in $. Weights are frozen at shift start and not re-optimised.
    2. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (no minimum throughput floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable production volume or revenue floor for this shift — for instance, at least 80 % of the baseline shift output — below which an efficiency gain would still be rejected even if the value-per-resource ratio improves? Without a floor the optimiser can maximise the ratio by drastically reducing scale, which may violate customer commitments.
       A: Impose a minimum throughput constraint of 85 % of the rolling 4-week average shift output for the same product mix; solutions below this floor are infeasible regardless of efficiency ratio.
    3. context.temporal_horizon (shift_duration unspecified)
       Q: What is the actual duration of this shift in hours (e.g. 8 h, 10 h, or 12 h)? Shift length directly changes the feasible fatigue accumulation trajectory and changes the regulatory interpretation of the 80 dB noise limit (ISO 9612 / OSHA TWA scales with exposure hours), so the same threshold value has different real-world meanings across shift lengths.
       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift. Fatigue sampling interval is set to 60 minutes (9 samples including start). Noise limit is interpreted as LAeq,8h per EU Directive 2003/10/EC; if actual shift length differs, the equivalent noise limit is recalculated before the optimiser runs.
    4. noise_level vs. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (feasibility conflict at high throughput)
       Q: Are there specific machine modes or cycle-rate settings that inherently exceed 80 dB and cannot be acoustically mitigated — and if so, should those modes be hard-excluded from the schedule, or is the noise limit negotiable (e.g. raised to 83 dB with mandatory PPE) if they are the only way to hit the throughput floor?
       A: Hard-exclude any operational mode whose measured noise exceeds 80 dB at the primary workstation; if no feasible schedule exists without those modes the optimiser raises an infeasibility alert to the manager before committing to a plan. The noise limit is not relaxed automatically.
    5. priority_order vs. _raw_priority_order (conflict between HumanWellbeing priority and efficiency direction)
       Q: When the optimiser faces a small efficiency gain (e.g. +2 % delivered value per resource) that would push operator fatigue from 0.35 to exactly 0.40 — still within the hard limit but close to it — should it prefer the efficiency gain or protect a fatigue buffer, and if a buffer is desired, what is the target ceiling (e.g. fatigue ≤ 0.35 as a soft goal)?
       A: Treat the hard constraint (fatigue ≤ 0.40) as the binding limit with no additional soft buffer. Within the feasible region the optimiser maximises delivered_value_per_resource_consumed without further penalising fatigue levels below 0.40. If a soft buffer is later specified it can be added as a penalty term without restructuring the contract.

