       A: Apply cost-normalised weights using the most recently recorded unit costs: energy (€/kWh), raw material (€/kg), and labour (€/hr). If cost data are unavailable, weight all three components equally (1/3 each) after min-max normalisation to the shift historical range.
    4. noise_level (threshold measurement method)
       Q: The 80 dB constraint is assumed to be LAeq averaged over the full 8-hour shift, meaning short bursts well above 80 dB could be averaged out and still satisfy the contract while violating instantaneous occupational exposure limits. Should the 80 dB limit also apply as a 15-minute rolling average or a per-event peak cap (e.g. no single event above 85 dB), or is the shift-average-only interpretation acceptable?
       A: Enforce 80 dB as the shift-long LAeq AND impose an additional 85 dB ceiling on any 15-minute rolling window to guard against brief exceedances being masked by the average, consistent with ISO 9612 short-period measurement guidance.
    5. operator_fatigue (dual role: minimize KPI + hard constraint ≤ 0.4)
       Q: Operator fatigue appears both as a 'minimize' KPI objective and as a hard constraint (≤ 0.4). Actively minimising fatigue (pushing toward 0.0) conflicts with maximising resource efficiency because slower pace and longer breaks reduce both. What is the practical lower target for fatigue — is fatigue simply a constraint to stay below 0.4, or is there a specific target band (e.g. 0.2–0.3) you want the schedule to aim for?
       A: Treat operator_fatigue purely as a hard upper-bound constraint (≤ 0.4); remove the 'minimize' objective direction to avoid a conflicting bi-criteria problem. The optimiser will prefer schedules with lower fatigue only when two solutions are otherwise equivalent in resource_efficiency.

